Then they brought little children to him that he might touch them; but the disciples rebuked those who brought them.
Then Jesus said, "Let the little children come to me, and do not hinder them, for to such belongs the kingdom of heaven."
Then Jesus laid his hands on them and left that place. The obstacle of wealth
A man came to Jesus and asked him, "Teacher, what good thing must I do to get eternal life?"
Jesus replied, "Why do you ask me about what is good? There is only one who is good. If you want to enter life, keep the commandments."
The man said, "Which commandments?" Jesus replied, "Don't murder, don't commit adultery, don't steal."
Do not bear false witness against your neighbor, honor your father and mother. #Do not kill…and your mother. 19:19 From Exodus 20:12-16 and Deuteronomy 5:16-20. And love your neighbor #your friend. 19:19 Referring to Luke's Gospel 10:25-37, we understand that "neighbor" means every person in need of help. As you love yourself. #love...yourself. 19:19 From Leviticus 19:18.
The young man said to him: "I obey all these commandments, so what else do I lack?"
Jesus said to him, "If you want to be perfect, go sell your possessions and give to the poor, and you will have treasure in heaven. Then come follow me."
When he heard that, the young man went away sad because he was very rich.
Then Jesus said to his disciples, "Truly I tell you, it is hard for someone who is rich to enter the kingdom of heaven."
I tell you again that it is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for someone who is rich to enter the kingdom of God.”
When his disciples heard this, they were greatly astonished and said, "Then who can be saved?"
Then Jesus looked at them and said to them, "With men this is impossible, but with God all things are possible."
Then Peter said to him, "We have left everything to follow you! What then will we have?"
Jesus said to them, "I tell you the truth, when the Son of Man sits on his glorious throne in the new world, you who have followed me will also sit on twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Israel."
And everyone who has left houses or brothers or sisters or father or mother or children or fields for my sake will receive a hundred times more and will inherit eternal life with God.”
Many will be among the first to become last, and many others who were last shall become first.
Like grape pickers
The kingdom of heaven is like a landowner who went out early in the morning to hire workers for his vineyard.
He agreed with his workers to pay them one denar for each day's work, then sent them off to work in his vineyard.
The owner of the vineyard left around nine o'clock and saw some men standing in the market area doing nothing.
He said to them, "Go you also into my vineyard, and I will give you what is right."
They went to work in the vineyard. "Again about an hour later he went out and found others standing around doing nothing. So he said to them, 'Why have you been hanging around all day?'"
Around five o'clock he went out again and found some others standing in the market place; he asked them why they had been idle all day long.
They said to him, "No one has hired us." He replied, "Go work in my vineyard too."
And at the end of the day, the landowner said to his manager: "Call the workers and pay them their wages, starting with those who were hired last and ending with those who were hired first."
Then came those who were hired about five o'clock in the afternoon; and they too received each man a denarius.
Then those who were hired first came thinking they would get more, but each one also received a denarius.
They took it away from him and started complaining about the innkeeper.
They say: "Those who were hired last worked just one hour, and you paid them the same amount we received, even though we toiled all day in the scorching sun!"
The innkeeper said to one of them: "I haven't wronged you my friend! Didn't we agree that you would work for me in exchange for one dinar?"
Take your pay and go. I want to give the last hired man the same wage I gave you.
Don't I have the right to do what I want with my own property? Or are you jealous because I'm getting along well with others?
"So those who think they stand must be careful not to fall." Jesus foretells his death and resurrection.
As Jesus was making his way to Jerusalem, he took the twelve aside and said to them:
We're going to Jerusalem where the Son of Man will be handed over to the chief priests and teachers of the law who will condemn him to death.
They will hand him over to non-believers who will mock him, beat him up, and crucify him. But on the third day he will rise from death." The greatest one is the servant.
Then his wife came to him with her two sons, and she prostrated herself before him asking for something.
Jesus said to her, "What do you want?" She replied, "Grant that these two sons of mine may sit, one at your right hand and one at your left, in your kingdom."
Jesus said to them, "Do you know what you're asking? Can you drink from the cup I am going to drink from?" They said to him, "We can."
He said to them, "My cup you will indeed drink, but to sit at My right hand or at My left is not Mine to give, but it is for those for whom it has been prepared by My Father."
When the other ten heard this request, they were really angry with the brothers.
Then Jesus called them to him and said, "You know that those who are regarded as rulers of the Gentiles lord it over them, and their high officials exercise authority over them."
But this is not to be among you; whoever wants to become great among you must be your servant.
Whoever wants to be first must serve everyone else.
"For even the Son of Man did not come to be served, but to serve, and to give his life as a ransom for many." Jesus heals two blind men.
As they were leaving Jericho, large crowds followed him.
And there were two blind men sitting by the roadside. When they heard that Jesus was passing by, they shouted, "Lord, Son of David, have mercy on us!"
People scolded them and told them to be quiet, but they raised their voices even more: "Lord, Son of David, have mercy on us."
Then Jesus called them and said to them, "What do you want me to do for you?"
They said to him, "Master, please open our eyes."
Then Jesus touched their eyes and immediately they could see again; and they followed him.
Long live the king!
And when they drew near to Jerusalem, and came to Bethphage, unto the mount of Olives, then sent Jesus two disciples, saying unto them,
He said to them, "Go into the village that is in front of you, and there you will find a colt tied up, which has never been ridden by anyone; untie it and bring it here."
And if anyone says anything to you both, say to him: "The Lord needs them; he will bring them back soon."
This happened so that what the prophet said would come true.
Tell the city of Zion, your beloved one: #The Beloved Zion. 5:21 Literally "the daughter of Zion." Here is your king coming to you, humble and riding on a donkey, a colt, the foal of a female donkey.
The two disciples went and did what Jesus said.
They brought him the colt and put their cloaks on it, and he sat on them.
Most people spread their cloaks on the road, but others cut branches from trees and laid them down for us to walk on.
The crowds that were walking ahead of him and behind him were shouting: "Long live the king!" #Long live the king. 9:21 Literally: "Hosanna." In Hebrew it means: "Save now." Most likely here it's a shout of praise to God and his anointed King. Repeated in verse 15. Long live the son of David. Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord. Long live the king in his glory."
When Jesus entered Jerusalem, the whole city was shaken. And people were asking, "Who is this man?"
And those who followed him were saying, "This is Jesus the prophet from Nazareth in Galilee." Jesus at the Temple
Then Jesus entered the temple courts and drove out all who were buying and selling there. He overturned the tables of the money changers and the seats of those selling doves.
He said to them: "It is written: 'My house will be called a house of prayer' #my house ... prayer. 13:21 from Isaiah 56:7. But you have made it a den of robbers!" #den of robbers. 13:21 from Jeremiah 7:11."
And some blind men and cripples came to him in the temple court, and he healed them.
The chief priests and teachers of religious law saw the wonderful things he had done and heard the children shouting in the temple courts, "Hosanna to the Son of David." But they were indignant. "Do you hear what these children are saying?" they asked him.
Jesus replied, "Yes, but have you never read in the Scriptures: 'From the lips of children and infants you have ordained praise'?"
Then Jesus left them and went away to the town of Bethsaida, where he spent the night. The power of faith
As Jesus was going along in the morning, he became hungry.
He saw a fig tree by the roadside, so he went up to it but found nothing but leaves; then he said to it: "May you never bear fruit again!" Immediately the fig tree withered away.
When the disciples saw this they were amazed and asked him, "Why was the fig tree dried up like that?"
He replied to them, "I tell you the truth, if you have faith and do not doubt, not only can you do what was done to the fig tree, but also if you say to this mountain, 'Be lifted up and thrown into the sea,' it will be done."
And whatever you ask for in prayer with faith, you will receive." Doubting Jesus' authority
Jesus went to the temple courts and was teaching. But the chief priests and the elders approached him and said, "By what authority are you doing these things? And who gave you this authority?"
Jesus answered them, "And I will also ask you one question; tell me, by what authority am I doing these things?"
Where did John's baptism come from? From God or from men?" They discussed this among themselves and said, "If we say it came from God, he will ask us, 'Why didn't you believe him?'"
If we say that she is one of us, then we fear people because they all consider John to be a prophet.
So they answered Jesus, "We don't know." Then Jesus said to them, "Neither will I tell you by what authority I am doing these things." The Parable of the Two Sons
