The son replied, "I don't want to go." But he changed his mind and went.
Then the father went to his other son and asked him for the same thing. The boy answered: "Yes sir, I'll go." But he didn't go.
Which son did what his father wanted? They replied, "The first one." Jesus said to them, "Truly I tell you, tax collectors and prostitutes are entering the kingdom of God ahead of you.
John the Baptist came to show you the way of truth, but you didn't believe him. But tax collectors and prostitutes believed in him. Even when you saw what they did, you still refused to repent and believe. It's like the parable of the vineyard owner
And another parable he put forth unto them, saying, The kingdom of heaven is like unto a man that was a householder, which went out early in the morning to hire labourers into his vineyard.
When it was time to harvest the grapes, he sent his servants to the farmers to get his share of the grapes.
But the peasants caught their slaves, beat one to death, killed another, and stoned yet another.
The owner sent more slaves than he had sent before. The peasants treated them in exactly the same way.
Finally he sent his son, saying to himself, "They'll respect my son."
But when the peasants saw that this was the owner's son, they consulted among themselves and said: "This is the heir; let us kill him so we can take his inheritance."
They arrested him and threw him outside the vineyard where they killed him.
So what do you think will happen to those farmers when he comes back?
They said to him: "He will destroy them in an awful way because they're wicked, then he'll give the vineyard to other farmers who will give him its fruit when it is ripe."
Jesus said to them, "Haven't you read what was written: 'The stone that the builders rejected has become the cornerstone.' The Lord has done this, and it is marvelous in our eyes."
That's why I tell you this: The kingdom of God will be taken away from you and given to a people who produce its fruit.
"Anyone who falls on this stone will be broken to pieces, and if anyone falls on him, he will be crushed!"
When the chief priests and Pharisees heard Jesus' parables, they knew he was talking about them.
So they tried to arrest him, but were afraid of the people who considered Jesus a prophet.
Like a wedding feast
And Jesus spoke to them again in parables, saying:
The kingdom of heaven is like a king who made a wedding banquet for his son.
The king sent his servants to call those who were invited to the wedding feast, but they did not want to come.
So he sent other servants to say to those invited, 'Tell those who have been invited that everything is ready; the oxen and fattened cattle have been killed, and all is prepared. Come to the wedding feast.'
But the guests didn't care about it at all; each one went his own way - one to his farm, another to his business.
The rest captured the king's slaves and beat them to death.
Then the king was angry and sent his army to kill those murderers and burn their city.
Then the king said to his servants, 'The wedding banquet is ready, but those invited were not worthy.'
So go to the street corners and invite everyone you find to come to the wedding feast.
They went out to the streets and gathered everyone they found, both good and bad people, until the banquet hall was filled with guests.
When the king came in to see the guests, he saw a man there who was not dressed for the wedding.
The king said to him: "Friend, how did you get in here without wedding clothes?" But the man remained silent.
The king said to his servants: "Tie up his hands and feet, throw him outside in the darkness where people will weep and gnash their teeth."
"For many call, but few are chosen." The Pharisees try to trap Jesus
So the Pharisees went away and plotted how they might trap Jesus in something he said.
They sent their disciples to him along with some men from Herod's party, saying, "Teacher, we know that you are true and teach God's way in line with truth, for you do not concern yourself with people's status or position."
Tell us what you think; is it right to pay taxes to Caesar or not?
But Jesus knew their thoughts and said to them, "Why do you think evil in your hearts?"
Show me the coin you use." They brought him a denarius.
He asked them: "Whose is this drawing and whose name is engraved on the coin?"
They said to him, "These belong to Caesar." He replied, "Then give back to Caesar what is Caesar's, and to God what is God's."
When they heard his answer, they were greatly astonished and left him alone and went away. The Sadducees try to trap Jesus
On that day some Sadducees came to him, who say there is no resurrection; and they asked him,
Teacher, Moses said #MosesSaid. Deuteronomy 24:22 Look at Deuteronomy 25:5, 6. If a man dies without children, his brother must marry his widow and have a child in his name.
We had seven brothers in all. The first one got married but died without having children. So his brother took over his widow.
And it happened to the second one, third one, all the way up to the seventh one.
After they all died, so did the woman.
Who among these seven will be your wife in the afterlife? You've married them all."
Jesus answered them, "Because you don't know the Scriptures or the power of God."
Understand that in eternal life after resurrection, people don't get married or marry off their daughters; they become like angels in heaven.
As for the resurrection of the dead, haven't you read what God said?
"I am the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob." #I ... Jacob. Exodus 32:22 from the book of Exodus 3:6. "For He is not the God of the dead but of the living."
When people heard this teaching they were amazed at his wisdom. The greatest commandment
When the Pharisees heard that Jesus had silenced the Sadducees, they came together.
A religious scholar tried to trap him by asking:
Hey man, what's the greatest commandment in the law?
فقال له يسوع: "تحب الرب إلهك بكل قلبك، وبكل نفسك، وبكل عقلك." # تحب الرب ... عقلك. تثنية 6: 5
This is the first and greatest commandment.
The second commandment is like the first one: "Love your neighbor #your_neighbor." 39:22 Referring to Luke's gospel 10:25-37, we understand that the term "neighbor" refers to every person in need of help. As you love yourself. #love_your_neighbor ... yourself. 39:22 From Leviticus 19:18.
The entire law and the books of the prophets relate to these two commandments." The Messiah is Lord David
As the Pharisees were gathered around him, Jesus asked them:
What do you think about Christ? Whose son is he?" The Pharisees answered him, "He's David's son."
So he said to them: "Then how did David call him 'Lord' when he was inspired by God?"
The Lord said to my lord: "Sit at My right hand until I make your enemies your footstool."
If David called Christ his Lord, how much more so will His son be?
No one could answer him; after that nobody dared to ask him another question.
Jesus criticizes religious leaders
Then Jesus spoke to the crowds and his disciples
He said: "The teachers of the law and the Pharisees have taken over Moses's seat."
So keep what they say to you and practice it, but don't do their deeds. Because they speak, but don't act according to what they say.
They burden people with heavy loads that they themselves don't want to carry.
All good works they do, they do so that people may see them. They display their piety, increasing the size of their tassels #tassels 5:23 Some Jews would write specific passages from the Bible and put them in small leather bags, then tie them with cloth tassels to their forehead or left arm, showing off their extreme devotion. And they lengthened the fringes of their garments.
They love sitting in the best seats at banquets and in the front rows at meetings.
They like to be greeted with special greetings in the markets, and they want people to call them "Ya M3alem" (O teacher).
But you must not be called Rabbi, for you have one teacher, and you are all brothers.
And do not call anyone your father here on earth, for you have one Father, who is in heaven.
Do not let people call you "sir," for you have one Master, who is Christ.
The greatest among you should be your servant.
Whoever raises their own status will be lowered by Allah, but whoever humbles themselves will have their status raised by Allah.
Woe to you, teachers of the law and Pharisees, you hypocrites! You shut the kingdom of heaven in men's faces. You yourselves do not enter, nor will you let those entering go in.
Woe to you, teachers of the law and Pharisees, you hypocrites! You shut off the kingdom of heaven from people. You don't go in yourselves, nor do you let others who are willing to enter go in. Woe to you, teachers of the law and Pharisees, you hypocrites! You travel over land and sea to win a single convert, and when he becomes one, you make him twice as much a son of hell as you are.
Woe to you, teachers of the law and Pharisees, you hypocrites! You travel over land and sea to win a single convert, and when he becomes one, you make him twice as much a son of hell as you are.
Woe to you, teachers of the law and Pharisees, you hypocrites! You give a tenth of your spices—mint and dill and cumin. But you have neglected the more important matters of the law—justice, mercy and faithfulness. You should have practiced the latter, without neglecting the former.
O you fools and blind men! Which is greater: the gold in the temple or the temple that sanctifies the gold?
And you say: "If he swears by the altar, it is not binding upon him to keep his oath; but if anyone swears by what is offered in sacrifice on the altar, then he is bound to keep it!"
Which is greater: the offering that is placed upon the altar, or the altar that makes the offering holy?
For if anyone swears by an altar, it means he is swearing by the altar and everything upon it.
If someone swears by the Kaaba, they swear by it and what is inside it.
If someone swears by heaven, they swear by Allah's throne and whoever is sitting upon it.
Woe to you, teachers of the law and Pharisees, you hypocrites! You give a tenth of your spices—mint, dill and cumin. But you have neglected the more important matters of the law—justice, mercy and faithfulness. You should have practiced the latter, without neglecting the former.
Oh you blind guides! You strain at gnats but swallow camels.
Woe to you, teachers of the law and Pharisees, you hypocrites! You clean the outside of the cup and dish, but inside they are full of greed and wickedness.
Oh you Pharisees! Cleanse first that which is within the cup and platter, that the outside also may be clean.
Woe to you, teachers of the law and Pharisees, you hypocrites! You are like whitewashed tombs, which look beautiful on the outside but on the inside are full of dead people's bones and everything unclean.
That's how you also appear righteous outwardly but inside you are full of hypocrisy and evil.
Woe to you, teachers of the law and Pharisees, you hypocrites! You build tombs for the prophets and decorate the graves of the righteous.
And you say: "If we had lived in our ancestors' time, we would have participated in killing the prophets."
By this you confirm that you are descendants of those who killed prophets.
Continue what your ancestors started.
O generation of vipers, how can ye escape the damnation of hell?
So I tell you that I will send prophets and wise men and teachers to you, but you will kill some of them and crucify others, and flog some in your synagogues and persecute them from town to town.
Therefore you will be held accountable for the blood of every innocent person killed on earth: from the blood of righteous Abel to the blood of Zechariah son of Berechiah. #Abel...Zechariah. 35:23 The first and last who were killed according to time and half of the books of the Old Testament. (See Genesis 4:8 and Chronicles II 24:20) Who was killed between the temple and the altar.
I tell you the truth, all these crimes will be punished by this generation. Jesus warns the people of Jerusalem
O Jerusalem, O Jerusalem, you who kill the prophets and stone those sent to you! How often I have longed to gather your children together, as a hen gathers her chicks under her wings, but you were not willing.
Your house will be left empty and abandoned!
For I tell you this, you will never see me again until you say, 'Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord.' #blessed ... The LORD. Psalm 118:26
Jesus foretells the destruction of the temple
Jesus left the temple area. And while he was walking away, his disciples came up to him and called his attention to the temple buildings.
Jesus said to them, "Do you see all these things? I tell you the truth, not one stone here will be left on another; every one will be thrown down."
While Jesus was sitting on the Mount of Olives, his disciples came to him privately and said, "Tell us, when will these things happen? And what is the sign that all these things are about to be fulfilled?"
Jesus replied to them, "Watch out so that you don't get deceived."
Many will come in my name, claiming to be Christ, and they will deceive many people.
"You will hear about wars and revolutions, but do not be afraid. Such things must happen, but they won't mean the end of the world."
Because nation will rise against nation, kingdom against kingdom. There will be earthquakes in various places, and famines.
But all these will be first pains of labor.
They will hand you over to be persecuted and killed, and all nations will hate you because of me.
At that time many will turn away from the faith and betray one another and hate each other.
And false prophets will appear and deceive many people.
And because of increased evil, many believers' love will grow cold.
But whoever remains faithful until the end will be saved.
And the gospel of the kingdom will be proclaimed throughout the whole world as a testimony to all nations, and then the end will come.
When you see "the abominable desolator" #النجس_المخرب. 15:24 Look at Daniel 9:27, 12:11, and also Matthew 11:31. The prophet Daniel referred to him standing in the holy place - so that readers can understand this statement
