I told you everything before it happened.
Someone might say to you, "Here is Jesus in the desert," but don't go there. Or he might say, "There he is inside one of the rooms," but don't believe him.
For just as lightning that comes from one part under heaven shines to the other part under heaven, so also the Son of Man will be in His day.
Wherever you find the corpse, you will also find vultures.
Immediately after the distress that will happen in those days, "the sun will be darkened, and the moon won't give its light. The stars will fall from heaven, and the heavenly bodies will be shaken."
At that time, the sign of the Son of Man will appear in heaven. Then all the tribes of the earth will mourn, and they will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of heaven with power and great glory.
He will send his angels with a loud trumpet blast, gathering those he has chosen from all directions - from one end of heaven to the other.
Learn from the fig tree; when its branches become tender and its leaves appear, you know that summer is near.
And when you see all these things, know that it is near - at the door. Compare with Luke 21:31.
I tell you the truth, this generation will certainly not pass away until all these things have happened.
The heavens and earth will pass away, but my words will never pass away. Only the Father knows.
But no one knows when that day or hour will come—neither the angels in heaven nor the Son; the Father alone knows.
As it was in the days of Noah, so will it be when the Son of Man comes.
In the days before the flood, people were eating, drinking, getting married, marrying off their daughters until that day when Noah entered the ark.
They did not know what would happen until the flood came and took them all away. So it will be at the coming of the Son of Man.
At that time, two men were working in a field, one was taken while the other was left behind.
And there were two women grinding grain at the millstone; one was taken away while the other was left behind.
So stay alert! You don't know when your master is coming.
Make sure that if the owner knew what time at night the thief was planning to come, he would wake up and stop him from robbing his house.
Therefore be also ready: for the Son of man cometh at an hour when ye think not. The good servant, and the evil servant
Who then is that faithful and wise servant, whom his master has made ruler over his household, to give them food in due season?
Congratulations to that servant who when his master comes finds him doing his duty.
I'm telling you the truth, he will inherit all his property.
But the wicked servant says to himself: "My master is taking his time."
He starts by beating his servants, then he eats and drinks with drunks.
So that master will come when you least expect him, at an hour you know nothing about.
He punishes him and places him among hypocrites where people cry and grind their teeth.
Like ten girls
Then the kingdom of heaven will be like ten virgins who took their lamps and went to meet the bridegroom.
Five of them are stupid, five of them are smart.
The stupid girls took their lamps but didn't take extra oil with them.
The smart ones took extra oil in their lamps along with the lights.
The groom was late, so all the girls fell asleep one by one.
But at midnight someone cried out, "Here comes the bridegroom! Go out to meet him!"
Then the girls woke up and prepared their lamps.
The foolish virgins said to the wise ones, "Give us some of your oil, for our lamps are going out."
The smart ones replied: "We can't, it isn't enough for us but... Go buy oil from the vendors yourselves."
While they were going to buy oil, the groom arrived. The wise ones were prepared, so they went in with him for the wedding feast. Then he shut the door.
And finally, the other girls came up to him and said, "Master! Master! Open the door for us!"
But he said to me: "I swear I don't know you!"
Therefore stay awake, for you do not know on what day your Lord is coming. Like those three servants.
Likewise, the kingdom of heaven is like a man who was going to travel abroad; he called his servants and entrusted them with his property.
He gave one of them five bags #five bags. 15:25 Literally "weights or talents" where a weight equals about 27 to 36 kilograms of gold, silver, or copper. Of money, he gave the second two bags, and the third one bag. He gave each according to his ability. Then he immediately traveled away.
So he who had received five bags immediately began to invest them in business, and soon earned five more bags.
And he who took both bags did exactly like the first one, and won two more bags.
The one who took just one bag went off and dug a hole in the ground where he hid his master's money.
And after a long time, the master of these slaves returned and began to settle accounts with them.
Then came the man who had won five bags, and he said to the Caliph: "O my lord! You gave me five bags; here are five more that I have won."
His master said to him: "Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful over a little; I will set you over much. Enter into the joy of your master."
Then came the man who had taken the two bags, saying: "Sir, you gave me two bags; here are two more I have won."
His master said to him: "Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful over a little; I will set you over much. Enter into the joy of your master."
Then came one who took just one bag, saying: "Sir, I know you're a hard man; for you reap where you never sowed, and gather where you scattered no seed."
I was afraid of you, so I hid your bag in the ground. Take back your money.
His master answered him: "You're an evil slave and lazy. Since you know that I harvest what I didn't plant and reap from fields where I didn't sow."
Why didn't you deposit my money in the bank so that when I came back, I could get it with interest?
So they took the bag from him and gave it to the owner of ten bags.
For whoever has will be given more, and they will have an abundance. But from whoever does not have, even what they have will be taken away.
But that useless slave was thrown outside, into the darkness where people weep and gnash their teeth. The Son of Man is the judge.
When the Son of Man comes in his glory with all his angels, he will sit on his glorious throne.
Then he will gather all nations before him, and separate one from another, as a shepherd separates his sheep from the goats.
He will put the sheep on his right hand, and the goats on the left.
Then the king will say to those on his right hand, 'Come, you who have been blessed by my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world.'
For I was hungry and you gave me food, I was thirsty and you gave me drink, I was a stranger and you welcomed me.
I was naked, so you clothed me. I was sick, so you cared for me. I was in prison, so you visited me.
The righteous will ask Him: "Lord! When did we ever see You hungry and feed You, or thirsty and give You drink?"
And when did we see you as a stranger and welcome you in, or naked and clothe you?
When did we ever see you sick or in prison and visit you?
The king said: "I tell you the truth, whatever you did for one of the least of these brothers of mine, you did for me."
Then the king says to those standing at his left hand: "Depart from me, you cursed ones, into the eternal fire prepared for the devil and his angels."
For I was hungry but you gave me nothing to eat. I was thirsty but you gave me nothing to drink.
I was a stranger but you didn't take me in. I was naked but you didn't clothe me. I was sick and imprisoned but you didn't visit me.
Then shall the wicked answer him, saying, Lord, when saw we thee an hungred, or athirst, or a stranger, or naked, or sick, or in prison, and did not minister unto thee?
The king replied: "I tell you this truth, when you did not do it for one of my least brothers, you did not do it for me."
And so the wicked go to eternal punishment, but the righteous go to eternal life.
Jewish leaders plan to kill Jesus
After Jesus had finished saying these things to his disciples...
You know that Easter is after tomorrow, and the Son of Man will be handed over to his enemies to be crucified.
The high priests and elders had gathered at Caiaphas's palace.
They planned to arrest Jesus by trickery and kill him.
They said: "We shouldn't do this during the festival, to avoid causing trouble among people." A woman poured perfume over Jesus
While Jesus was in Bethany at Simon's house, who had leprosy...
A woman came to him with an expensive perfume in a marble bottle, which she poured over his head while he was eating.
When his disciples saw this they were indignant and said to him, "Why this waste?"
"This perfume could have been sold for a large sum of money to be given to the poor."
Jesus knew what they were saying about her, so he said to them, "Why do you trouble this woman? She has done something beautiful for me."
The poor you will have with you always, #thepoor...always. 11:26 See Deuteronomy 15:11. But I won't be with you forever.
I poured perfume over my body to prepare it for burial.
I tell you the truth, wherever this gospel is preached throughout the world, what she has done will also be told, in memory of her.” Judas Iscariot betrays Jesus
Then one of the twelve, whose name was Judas Iscariot, went to the chief priests.
He said to them, "What will you give me if I hand Jesus over to you?" They offered him thirty pieces of silver.
From that moment on, Judas began looking for an opportunity to hand Jesus over to them. At the Passover table
On the first day of Unleavened Bread, when it was customary to sacrifice the Passover lamb, the disciples came to Jesus and asked him, "Where do you want us to prepare for you to eat the Passover?"
Jesus said to them, "Go to so-and-so in the village and tell him, 'The Teacher says my appointed time is near. I am going to celebrate the Passover with my disciples at your place.'"
The disciples did what Jesus told them to do, and they prepared for Passover dinner.
When evening came, Jesus was reclining at table with his twelve disciples.
And while they were eating, he said to them, "Truly I tell you, one of you will betray me."
They were sad and started asking Him one by one: "Is it me?"
Jesus replied, "The one who dips his hand in the bowl with me will betray me."
"But it is written about the Son of Man what treatment he will receive. Woe to that man who betrays the Son of Man! It would be better for him if he had never been born!"
Judas, who was about to betray him, asked Jesus, "Is it me?" And Jesus replied, "Yes, you have said so." The Lord's Supper
And while they were eating, he took bread, gave thanks, broke it, and gave it to them saying, "Take this; this is my body."
Then he took up a cup of wine, gave thanks, and said to them all, "Drink from this, all of you."
For this is my blood of the covenant, which is poured out for many for forgiveness of sins.
And I tell you that I will never drink this wine until that day when I shall drink it new in my Father's kingdom."
Then they recited some psalms and went to the Mount of Olives. Jesus foretold Peter's denial
Then Jesus said to them, "All you will fall away because of me this night, for it is written: 'I will strike the shepherd, and the sheep of the flock will be scattered.'"
But after I rise from the dead, I will go ahead of you to Galilee.”
But Peter replied to him, "Even if all men forsake you, yet I will not forsake you."
Jesus said to him, "Truly I tell you, this very night, before the rooster crows twice, you will deny me three times."
Peter said to him, "Even if I have to die with you, I will never deny you!" And all the disciples spoke like this. Jesus was praying alone.
Then Jesus went with them to a place called Gethsemane, and he said to his disciples, "Sit here while I go over there and pray."
And he took with him Peter and James Zebedee's son, and began to feel sad and upset.
Then he said to them, "My soul is overwhelmed with sorrow to the point of death. Stay here and keep watch with me."
And Jesus withdrew from them about a stone's throw, and knelt down and prayed, saying, "Father, if you are willing, remove this cup from me; yet, not my will but yours be done."
He came to his disciples and found them sleeping. "Couldn't you men keep watch with me for one hour?" he asked Peter.
Stay awake and pray so that you will not fall into temptation. The spirit is willing, but the flesh is weak.”
And he withdrew again to pray, saying, "My Father, if this cannot pass unless I drink it, may Thy will be done."
Then he came back again and found them asleep because their eyes were heavy with sleep.
He left them again and went for the third time to pray, saying the same words he had said before.
Then he returned to his disciples and woke them up, saying, "Are you still sleeping and resting? Look, now is the time; soon the Son of Man will be delivered into the hands of sinners."
Let's go. Here comes the man who betrayed me." The arrest of Jesus
And while he was still speaking, behold, Judas, one of the twelve, came up to him with a great crowd of people with swords and clubs, who had been sent by the chief priests and elders of the people.
The traitor had given them a sign and said: "Whoever I kiss is the man you want, so arrest him."
Then he came up to Jesus and said, "Good morning, Teacher." And he bowed down to him and touched his feet.
Jesus said to him, "Friend, do what you came for." Then they stepped forward, seized Jesus and arrested him.
One of those with Jesus reached for his sword, drew it, and struck the high priest's slave, cutting off his ear.
Jesus said to him, "Put your sword back in its place; for all who take the sword will perish by the sword."
Don't you realize that I could call upon my Father right now, and he would send me more than twelve legions of angels at once?
But if you do, how will those books that said these things would happen come true?
At that moment Jesus said to the crowds: "Did you come out against me with swords and clubs like when you arrest a criminal? I was teaching every day in the temple courts, but you didn't seize me."
But this happened to fulfill what was written through the prophets." Then all the disciples deserted him and fled. Jesus before Jewish leaders
Then those who had arrested Jesus took him to Caiaphas, the high priest's house, where the teachers of the law and the elders had assembled.
But Peter followed him at a distance, right into the courtyard of the high priest's house. And he stood there with the guards to see how it would all end.
The chief priests and all their council were looking for some testimony against Jesus so that they might put him to death.
But they couldn't find him, even though many false witnesses came forward and made up lies about him. Finally, two men stepped forward...
They said, "This man—this Jesus—whose enemies never mentioned his name!" He had claimed to be able to destroy God's temple and rebuild it in three days.
The high priest stood up and said to him, "Have you no answer to make? What do these men testify against you?"
But Jesus remained silent. Then the high priest said to him, "I adjure you by the living God that you tell us if you are the Christ, the Son of God."
Jesus replied, "Yes, I am he." Then he added, "But from now on you will see the Son of Man sitting at the right hand of the Mighty One and coming on the clouds of heaven."
Then the high priest tore his clothes and said, "He has blasphemed! Why do we need witnesses? You have now heard his blasphemy."
"So what do you think?" they replied, "He deserves death."
Then they spat in his face and hit him with their fists and slapped him.
They were saying to him, "Prophesy! Who is it that struck you?" Peter denies Jesus
في الوقت ده، بطرس كان قاعد برة في الساحة. وبعدين جتله واحدة من خدامين كبير الكهنة وقالت له: "يا راجل إنت كمان كنت مع يسوع الناصري."
But Peter denied it in front of everyone and said, "I don't know what you're talking about!"
Then he went to the gate of the courtyard, when another servant said to those who were there: "This man was with Jesus from Nazareth."
But Peter denied it again; and after a little while began to curse and swear, saying, "I do not know this man."
And after a little while those who were standing there said to him, "Surely you are one of them; for your accent betrays you."
Then he began to curse #curse 74:26 - swearing by cursing if he was lying! He swore and said: "I don't know this man." And then the rooster crowed.
Then Peter remembered what Jesus had said to him: "Before the rooster crows you will disown me three times." So he went outside and wept bitterly.
Jesus before Pilate
The next morning, the chief priests and elders met to plot Jesus's death.
They arrested him, led him away, and handed him over to Governor Pilate. Judas committed suicide.
When Judas Iscariot saw that they had decided to condemn Jesus to death, he regretted what he did. So he returned the thirty pieces of silver to the chief priests and elders.
He said to them, "I have made a mistake in delivering an innocent man to be killed." They replied, "What is that to us? You must deal with this matter yourself."
Judas threw down the silver coins in the temple and left; then he went away and hanged himself.
The chief priests took the coins and said, "It is not lawful to put this money in the temple treasury because it was blood money."
They decided to buy the potter's field with it so that it could be used as a burial ground for strangers.
And that's why this field is still known today as "The Blood Field."
And thus was fulfilled what God said through Jeremiah the prophet: "They took the thirty pieces of silver, which were the price set by the people of Israel."
They bought the potter's field with it, just as the Lord had instructed me." #they took...the Lord. 10:27 See Zechariah 11:12-13 and Jeremiah 32:6-9. Jesus before Pilate
Jesus stood before the governor, who asked him, "Are you the king of the Jews?" Jesus answered, "It is as you say."
When the chief priests and elders complained about him, he said nothing.
Then Pilate asked him, "Aren't you going to answer these charges they are bringing against you?"
But Jesus did not give Pilate an answer to anything he was accused of. So Pilate was amazed at his silence. Pilate's failure to release Jesus
During Easter, it was customary for the governor to release one prisoner chosen by the people.
And there was one named Barabbas, who had been imprisoned with his comrades for taking part in an insurrection that had led to murder. #Barabbas 16:27 Or "Jesus Barabbas" as found in some Greek manuscripts.
When they had gathered together, Pilate said to them, "Whom do you want me to release for you? Jesus who is called Christ, or Barabbas?"
Pilate knew that they had handed Jesus over to him because of their jealousy.
While Pilate was sitting in judgment, his wife sent him word saying, "Have nothing to do with that innocent man; for I have suffered much over a dream about him today."
But the chief priests and elders persuaded the crowds to ask for Barabbas's release instead of Jesus'.
The governor said, "Which one do you want me to release?" They replied, "Barabbas."
Then Pilate asked them, "What shall I do then with Jesus who is called Christ?" They all said to him, "Let Him be crucified."
Then Pilate said to them, "Why? What crime has he committed?" But they shouted all the louder, "Crucify him!"
When Pilate saw that his efforts were in vain and that disorder was breaking out, he took some water and washed his hands before the crowd. "I am innocent of this man's blood," he said to them. "It is your responsibility."
The whole people answered and said, "His blood be upon us and upon our children."
Then Pilate handed him over to them to be crucified. So they took Jesus, and he went out, bearing his own cross, to the place called The Skull, which in Aramaic is called Golgotha. There they crucified him, and with him two others, one on either side, with Jesus between them. Pilate also had an inscription written and put on the cross. It read, "Jesus of Nazareth, the King of the Jews." Many of the Jews read this inscription, for the place where Jesus was crucified was near the city; and it was written in Hebrew, Latin, and Greek. Then the chief priests of the Jews protested to Pilate, "Do not write, 'The King of the Jews,' but rather, 'This man said, I am King of the Jews.'" Pilate answered, "What I have written I have written." When the soldiers had crucified Jesus, they took his clothes and divided them into four parts, one for each soldier. They also took his tunic, which was seamless, woven in one piece from top to bottom. So they said to one another, "Let us not tear it, but cast lots for it to see whose it shall be." This was to fulfill what was spoken through the prophet: "They divide my garments among themselves, and for my clothing they cast lots." Therefore the soldiers did these things. But standing by the cross of Jesus were his mother, and his mother's sister, Mary the wife of Clopas, and Mary Magdalene. When Jesus saw his mother, and the disciple whom he loved standing nearby, he said to his mother, "Woman, behold, your son!" Then he said to the disciple, "Behold, your mother!" And from that hour the disciple took her to his own home. After this, when Jesus knew that all was now finished, he said (in order to fulfill the scripture), "I thirst." A jar full of sour wine stood there, so they put a sponge full of the wine on a hyssop branch and held it to his mouth. When Jesus had received the vinegar, he said, "It is finished," and bowed his head and gave up his spirit. Since it was the day of Preparation, and so that the bodies would not remain on the cross on the Sabbath (for that Sabbath was a high day), the Jews asked Pilate to have the legs broken and the bodies taken away. So the soldiers came and broke the legs of the first, and of the other who had been crucified with him. But when they came to Jesus and saw that he was already dead, they did not break his legs. But one of the soldiers pierced his side with a spear, and at once there came out blood and water. He who saw it has borne witness—his testimony is true; and he knows that he tells the truth—that you also may believe. For these things happened that the scripture might be fulfilled: "Not one of his bones will be broken." And again another scripture says, "They will look on him whom they have pierced." After this, Joseph of Arimathea, who was a disciple of Jesus, though secretly because of his fear of the Jews, asked Pilate if he could take away the body of Jesus. Pilate gave him permission; so he came and took away his body. Nicodemus also, who earlier had come to Jesus by night, came bringing a mixture of myrrh and aloes, about seventy-five pounds in weight. So they took the body of Jesus and bound it in linen cloths with the spices, as is the burial custom of the Jews. Now in the place where he was crucified there was a garden, and in the garden a new tomb in which no one had yet been laid. So because of the Jewish day of Preparation, since the tomb was close at hand, they laid Jesus there.
Then the governor's soldiers took Jesus to the palace and gathered all his guards around him.
They stripped him naked then dressed him in a scarlet robe. #dressed… color 28:27 Or purple, mocking him, because this is the color of royal robes.
They wove for him a crown of thorns and put it on his head, and they put a staff in his right hand. Then they knelt down before him and mocked him. "Hail, king of the Jews!" they said.
Then they spat in his face, took away the reed from him, and started hitting him on the head.
When they had finished mocking him, they stripped off his clothes and put his own clothes back on him. Then they led him away to crucify him. Jesus on the cross
When they had finished with him, they found Simon from Cyrene, who was coming in from the country, and forced him to carry his cross.
When they came to a place called Golgotha, which means "the place of the skull,"
They gave Jesus wine mixed with myrrh to drink, but when he tasted it, he refused to drink it.
When they crucified Jesus, they divided his clothes among them and cast lots for them.
Then they sat there guarding him.
They put up over his head an inscription with the title that had been fixed by him: "This is Jesus, king of the Jews."
Two criminals were crucified with Jesus, one on his right hand and another on his left.
The Maronites were cursing him and shaking their heads
They say to him, "If you are God's son, come down from the cross!"
And so the chief priests and teachers of religious law and elders mocked him.
He can save others but cannot save himself! He is the king of Israel! So let him come down from the cross now so that we may believe in Him!
He put his trust in God; now let Him save him if He will." "Didn't he say: 'I am the Son of God'?"
And those who were crucified with him also taunted him in the same way. The death of Jesus
From noon until three in the afternoon, darkness covered all the earth.
And around three o'clock Jesus cried out in a loud voice, "Eli, Eli, lema sabaktani?" #Eli... why have you forsaken me? 46:27 From Psalm 22:1 Meaning: "My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?"
When some of those standing there heard this, they said, "He's calling for Elijah!" #Calling for Elijah. 47:27 The word "Elijah" in Hebrew is pronounced "Eliyah," while in Aramaic it's "Elo." It resembles the name "Elias," which was the name of a well-known prophet who lived around 850 BC.
Then one of those standing there ran up to him with a sponge full of vinegar on the end of a stick, so that he could be given some to drink.
The others said, "Let's wait to see if Elijah will come save him!"
Then Jesus shouted again in a loud voice, and gave up his spirit.
The temple curtain was torn in two from top to bottom. The earth shook, rocks split...
The tombs were opened, and many holy bodies of believers who had died rose up.
After Jesus rose from death, those bodies came back to life, entered the holy city, and appeared to many people.
But the Roman officer, #RomanOfficer 54:27 Literally "commander of one hundred." And the guards who were guarding Jesus' body saw the earthquake and other events, they became terrified and said: "This was truly God's Son!"
And there were also women looking on from afar, who had followed Him from Galilee, ministering to Him.
Among them were Mary Magdalene, Mary mother of James and Joseph, and also the mother of Zebedee's sons. #Zebedee's sons 56:27 James and John. The burial of Jesus
When evening came, a rich man named Joseph from Arimathea arrived in Jerusalem. He too was one of Jesus' disciples.
He went to Pilate and asked for Jesus' body. So Pilate ordered that it be given him.
Joseph took the body and wrapped it in new linen cloth.
Then he put him in his new grave that he had dug in the rock, then rolled a huge stone over the entrance to the tomb and left.
And Mary Magdalene and the other Mary were sitting opposite the tomb. Guarding Jesus's tomb
The next day, after Friday was over, the chief priests and Pharisees met with Pilate.
They said to him: "Sir, we remember that this deceiver predicted before his death: 'I will rise from the dead after three days.'"
So he gave orders for the tomb to be made secure until the third day, so that his disciples might not come and steal the body and then tell the people, "He has been raised from the dead." In this way there would be another evil than the first.
Then Pilate said to them, "Take a guard of soldiers from among you, and go make the tomb as secure as you know how."
They went to fix the tomb. They put a seal on the stone and posted guards from the army there.
Resurrection of Jesus
After the Sabbath ended, at dawn on the first day of the week, Mary Magdalene and the other Mary went to look at the tomb.
At that moment there was a great earthquake; for an angel of the Lord descended from heaven and came and rolled back the stone and sat upon it.
And he was shining like lightning, and his clothes were white as snow.
The guards were terrified of him and became like dead men.
The angel said to the women, "Don't be afraid! I know you are looking for Jesus who was crucified."
He is not here; for he has risen, just as he said. Come and see the place where they laid him.
Then go quickly and tell his disciples that he has risen from the dead; and behold, he is going before you to Galilee; there you will see him. See, I have told you.”
Then both women ran away quickly from the tomb, because they were filled with fear and joy. They rushed to tell his disciples what had happened.
Suddenly Jesus met them and said, "Greetings!" They came up to him, took hold of his feet, and worshiped him.
Jesus said to them, "Do not be afraid; go tell my brothers to leave for Galilee; there they will see me." The false report
While the two women were walking back to town, some guards went ahead and told all the high priests what had happened.
The high priests gathered with the elders, consulted among themselves, then gave the soldiers a large sum of money
They said to them, "Spread it among the people that while you were sleeping, Jesus' disciples came at night and stole his body."
"If this news reaches the governor, we'll convince him, and you'll be safe."
The soldiers took the money and did what they were told. And so this story spread among Jews until today. Jesus speaks to his disciples
The eleven disciples went to Galilee, to the mountain where Jesus had told them to go.
When they saw him, they bowed down to worship him, even though some had doubts about it.
Then Jesus came to them and said, "All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to me."
Go therefore and make disciples of all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit.
Teach them to obey everything I have commanded you. Remember that I will be with you always, even until the end of time."
John the Baptist
This is the beginning of the good news about Jesus Christ, God's Son.
As it is written in the book of Isaiah the prophet: "Behold I send my messenger before thy face, which shall prepare thy way before thee."
A voice cries out in the wilderness: "Prepare the way for the Lord; make straight paths for him."
John the Baptist came to baptize in the wilderness, calling people to repent and be baptized for forgiveness of their sins.
All residents from Judea's villages and Jerusalem came to him. He baptized them in the Jordan River after they confessed their sins.
His clothes were made from camel hair, he wore a leather belt around his waist, and ate locusts and wild honey.
And he proclaimed, saying, "He who comes after me is mightier than I, whose sandals I am not worthy to carry."
"I baptize you with water; but he will baptize you with the Holy Spirit." Baptism of Jesus
In those days Jesus came from Nazareth in Galilee and was baptized by John in the Jordan River.
As he came up out of the water, he saw heaven being opened and the Spirit descending on him like a dove.
A voice came from heaven: "This is my Son, whom I love; with him I am well pleased." Jesus's experience
And he led him into the wilderness by himself.
And he was there forty days being tempted by Satan. He was with wild animals, and angels attended him. Jesus chose some of his disciples
After John was arrested, Jesus came to Galilee proclaiming the good news of God.
And he says: "The time has come; the kingdom of God is near at hand. Repent and believe in this good news."
As he was walking along the shore of Lake Galilee, he saw Simon and his brother Andrew casting their nets in the lake, for they were fishermen.
Jesus said to them, "Follow me, and I will make you fishers of men."
They immediately closed their windows and followed him.
Then he walked a little farther and saw James son of Zebedee and his brother John in their boat preparing to go out fishing with their father Zebedee. So Jesus called them.
Jesus called them; they left their father Zebedee in the boat with the hired men and followed him. Jesus drives out an evil spirit
Then they went to Capernaum, and Jesus entered the synagogue on the Sabbath day and began teaching.
They were amazed by his teaching because he taught them like someone who had authority, unlike our religious scholars.
And there was a man in the synagogue who had an unclean spirit; and he cried out,
What do you want from us, Jesus of Nazareth? Have you come to destroy us? I know who you are—you are the Holy One of God.
Jesus rebuked him and said to him, "Mute! Come out of him!"
The unclean spirit threw the man into convulsions, then he screamed loudly and came out of him.
Everyone was amazed and started saying to each other: "What is this new teaching? It gives authority over evil spirits so they obey him."
The news about him spread quickly throughout all Judea and Galilee. Jesus healed many people.
Then they left the crowd and went with James and John to Simon and Andrew's house.
Simon's mother-in-law was in bed with a fever, so they told Jesus about her.
He came closer to her, took hold of her hand, and made her sit down. The fever left her, and she started serving them.
That evening at sunset they brought him many patients who were possessed by evil spirits.
All the people in the city gathered at the house's door.
Jesus healed many who were suffering from various diseases, and cast out many demons. But Jesus did not allow the spirits to speak because they knew who he was. The readiness for proclaiming the Gospel
Early in the morning, while it was still dark, Jesus got up, left the house and went off to a solitary place, where he prayed.
Simon and those with him went to look for Him.
When they found him, they said to him, "Everyone is looking for you!"
Jesus replied to them, "Let us go somewhere else—to the nearby villages—so I can preach there also. That is why I have come."
He went throughout Galilee preaching in their synagogues and driving out demons. Jesus heals lepers
A leper came to Jesus and knelt down before him, begging him and saying, "If you want to, you can make me clean."
Then Jesus touched him and said, "I will; be clean." And immediately his leprosy was cleansed.
The toad left the man's leg, and he became pure.
Then Jesus warned him sternly before sending him away
And he said to him, "Do not tell anyone what has happened to you, but go show yourself to the priest; #go ... to the priest. 44:1 The priest was responsible for determining according to the law when someone with leprosy could be considered clean. Offer the offering for your purification that Moses commanded, #that Moses commanded. 44:1 See Leviticus 14:1-32. So people will know that you have been healed."
But the man went away and began to spread the news about his healing everywhere, so that it became impossible for Jesus to enter a town openly; he had to stay outside in lonely places, where people came to him from every direction.
Jesus heals a paralyzed man
After several days, Jesus returned to Capernaum, and news spread about his return.
Many people gathered there until there was no more room, even outside the door. And Jesus spoke to them about God's word.
They brought to him a paralyzed man carried by four men.
But they couldn't get him to Jesus because of the crowd, so they dug through the roof above where Jesus was and lowered the mat with the paralyzed man lying on it.
When Jesus saw their faith, he said to the paralyzed man, "Son, your sins are forgiven."
And some religious scholars were sitting there, thinking to themselves:
Why does this man speak like that? He's insulting God with his words! Who else can forgive sins except for God himself?
But Jesus knew their thoughts and said to them, "Why do you think these things in your hearts?"
Which is easier to say: "Your sins are forgiven" or "Get up, take your mat and walk"?
لكن أنا هوريك إن ابن الإنسان عنده سلطان على الأرض إنه يسامح الخطايا." وبعدين قال للشخص اللي كان مشلول:
I tell you, get up, take your mat and go home!
He got up immediately, picked up his mat, and walked in front of everyone. They were all amazed and praised God, saying, "We have never seen anything like this before!" Levi (Matthew) follows Jesus
Jesus went back to the lake again. He was teaching the crowds that had followed him there.
As he walked along, he saw Levi sitting at his tax booth, and said to him, "Follow me." And leaving everything behind, he got up and followed him.
While Jesus was having dinner at Levi's house, many tax collectors and sinners were eating with him and his disciples. Many people had been there when Jesus called Levi, so they followed Jesus.
When the Pharisees and teachers of religious law saw him eating with tax collectors and sinners, they asked his disciples, "Why does he eat with such scum?"
When Jesus heard this, he said to them, "Healthy people don't need a doctor—sick people do. I have come to call sinners, not righteous people." Question about fasting
And it was time for fasting for John's disciples and the Pharisees, so some people came to Jesus and asked him: "Why do your disciples fast but not ours?"
Jesus said to them, "Can you make wedding guests fast while the groom is with them? As long as they have the groom with them, they cannot fast."
But there will come a time when they will take away their bridegroom from them; then they shall fast.
No one sews new cloth onto an old garment, because the patch will shrink and tear the whole thing, making it worse.
No one pours new wine into old wineskins; otherwise, the wine will burst the skins, and both the wine and the skins will be ruined. Instead, new wine is poured into fresh wineskins.” Jesus: Lord of the Sabbath
On one Sabbath day Jesus was going through some fields, and his disciples began to pick some heads of grain while walking along with him.
The Pharisees said to Jesus, "Look, your disciples are doing what is unlawful on the Sabbath."
He said to them, "Haven't you read in the book what David did when he was hungry and his companions were with him?"
He entered God's house during Abiathar's time as priest, ate from the bread presented to God, and also gave some to those with him. Although it is forbidden for anyone except priests to eat that bread." #26:2 See First Samuel 21:1-6.
Then Jesus said to them, "The Sabbath was made for man, not man for the Sabbath."
And so the Son of Man is Lord even of the Sabbath."
Jesus heals on Saturday
Jesus went back to the temple again, where there was a man with a paralyzed hand.
And some people were watching him closely to see if he would heal him; they looked for an excuse to accuse him.
He said to the man with the shriveled hand, "Get up! Come here!"
Then he said to them: "Is it permissible to do good or evil on Saturday? Is it permissible to save someone's life or kill him?" They remained silent.
Jesus looked around at them in anger and was distressed by their hard hearts. Then he said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." He stretched it out, and his hand was completely restored.
The Pharisees went out and began to plot with Herod's followers how they might kill Jesus. Many were following Jesus.
Jesus went with his disciples to Lake Galilee, and there was a large crowd from Galilee and also from Judea.
And from Jerusalem and Idumea and beyond the Jordan and all around Tyre and Sidon, because they had heard about his miracles, came in great numbers to him.
Jesus asked his disciples to prepare a boat for him so that he wouldn't be crowded by the people.
Since Jesus had healed many people, everyone who was sick tried to reach him so they could touch him.
And unclean spirits would come out before him, crying out, "You are the Son of God!"
He warns her strongly against revealing who he is. The choice of twelve
Then Jesus went up on a mountainside and sat down with his disciples.
Jesus chose twelve men and called them apostles so that they could be with him and he could send them to preach in different places.
And they were given authority to drive out demons.
They called him Simon, but Jesus gave him the name Peter.
James son of Zebedee and his brother John, whom Jesus called "Boanerges" - meaning "Sons of Thunder."
Andrew, Philip, Bartholomew, Matthew, Thomas, James son of Alphaeus, Simon the Zealot, #Zealot. 18:3 From an Aramaic word meaning "the zealous one." It refers to a Jewish political party that resisted Roman rule called the Zealots.
Judas Iscariot who betrayed him. Jesus' power came from God and he returned home.
The people gathered around him and his disciples again until they didn't even have time to eat.
When Jesus' family heard he was home, they went to take custody of him because people were saying that he was crazy!
But the teachers of religious law who came from Jerusalem said, "It's by Beelzebul, the prince of demons, that he drives out demons."
Then Jesus called them to him and said, "How can Satan drive out Satan?
Because if a kingdom is divided against itself, it will not stand.
If a house is divided against itself, it cannot stand.
